57
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:50,720
A simple diffusion method for the distribution of ultra-fine silver bromide grains in precoated gelatin and customized polymeric films for holographic recording has been developed.

58
00:07:50,720 --> 00:07:53,360
The method involves two steps.

59
00:07:53,360 --> 00:08:04,960
Immersion of the preformed film in a solution of a silver salt, followed by agitation of the film in a solution of a bromide salt containing sensitizing dye.

60
00:08:04,960 --> 00:08:15,760
Repetition of the operation on the same film with different sensitizing dyes in the bromide bath enables the production of a film with panchromatic response.

61
00:08:15,760 --> 00:08:23,120
Transmission electron microscopy reveals a grain structure very suitable for use in holography.

62
00:08:23,120 --> 00:08:38,160
A reflection hologram made by this method has very similar brightness under the same exposure and processing conditions to one made from a proprietary hologram recording material currently available.

63
00:08:45,160 --> 00:09:05,640
Wavelength the plate is sensitive to, or having it possibly sensitive to both wavelengths of both red and green, and having the final results very similar in quality to materials that you're paying for and possibly exceeding.

64
00:09:05,640 --> 00:09:07,000
Let's not leave that out.

65
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:15,000
I'm sure as people begin working with this, they will find ways of actually taking it to another level as well.

66
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:19,720
Let's move on to the excuse me, uh, introduction.

67
00:09:19,720 --> 00:09:35,800
The original method conceived by Gabriel Lippmann over a century ago to produce the first filterless color photographs used in the constructive interference of each wavelength in the white light when it was returned from a reflective surface in intimate contact with the emulsion.

68
00:09:36,040 --> 00:09:43,000
The system gained a somewhat notorious reputation for making difficult demands on makers of silver halide emulsions.

69
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:49,400
The emulsions needed to be both exclusively of ultra-fine grain and panchromatic.

70
00:09:49,400 --> 00:10:00,120
Such fine-grained emulsions have seen a renaissance in the last 30 years with the development of holography, since the grain sizes for both techniques are comparable.

71
00:10:00,120 --> 00:10:16,120
However, commercially available silver halide emulsions materials intended for holography have been restricted to a relatively narrow band photosensitivity and have been generally unsuitable for the much more stringent demands of Lippmann's photographic method.

72
00:10:16,120 --> 00:10:23,400
The long-held impression that the fabrication of do-it-yourself Lipman emulsions is not for the faint-hearted may still linger.

73
00:10:23,400 --> 00:10:29,800
Consequently, there is a need for the introduction of a simple technique for making ultra-fine grain emulsions.

74
00:10:29,800 --> 00:10:42,920
This paper describes a diffusion method for producing a holographic recording material which avoids the traditional laborist emulsion forming methods for producing silver bromide in gelatin films.

75
00:10:42,920 --> 00:10:54,000
Light sensitive, ultra fine silver bromide grains can be introduced into thin films of a number of natural or synthetic polymers, even if they are somewhat hydrophobic.

76
00:10:54,000 --> 00:11:02,880
These polymers may be preformed or polymerized in SUTU by UV irradiation on a suitable substrate.

77
