00:18:05,840 --> 00:18:17,360
Put plate in cold solution of chromium acetate for one minute, shake off drips, and then without washing away that salt, blow plate with a cold air until dry.

160
00:18:17,360 --> 00:18:27,200
Once the layer is dry, leave the plate to complete the chrome hardening effect overnight in a warmer, preferably around 60 degrees Celsius for several hours.

161
00:18:27,200 --> 00:18:31,920
Rinse the hardened plate in distilled water and dry in a warm airflow.

162
00:18:31,920 --> 00:18:46,480
If you want to cut plate up for next step after scouring, scoring the glass on the back and cracking it, it is best not to pull sections apart before running a scapel blade along the gelatin side first so that it is cut and not torn apart.

163
00:18:46,480 --> 00:18:50,640
Once again, similar to taking the plate out of that coating bath.

164
00:18:50,640 --> 00:18:55,440
If you're going to score and cut the plates, just don't cut the glass side and then pull them apart.

165
00:18:55,440 --> 00:19:04,320
You might want to score and cut the gel also and have a clean cut on the gelatin side as well, which I think that it's getting at.

166
00:19:04,320 --> 00:19:10,320
Okay, the loading operation, silver bromide loading operation, and we're getting close to the end here.

167
00:19:10,640 --> 00:19:25,840
For 4x5 inch plate, place approximately 3 milliliters 6% silver nitrite solution in the center, and at once squash it with a clean flat cover plate, preferably transparent plastic so that you can see air bubbles are squeezed out.

168
00:19:25,840 --> 00:19:27,600
Leave for three minutes.

169
00:19:27,600 --> 00:19:32,200
Safe lighting is not necessary here, but the lighting should be subdued.

170
00:19:29,600 --> 00:19:41,800
Remove cover plate and immediately remove the excess silver solution on its surface by gently brushing over the plate with the edge of a piece of filter paper.

171
00:19:41,800 --> 00:19:44,600
Blow dry plate with cool air.

172
00:19:44,600 --> 00:19:53,080
And under safe light conditions, add 2.5 milliliter of dye solution per 100 milliliter of lithium bromide solution.

173
00:19:53,080 --> 00:20:02,440
Add about 0.2 milliliter of 1% ascorbic acid solution, and this is the same solution as used in the final sensitizing bath.

174
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:08,360
Agitate the bath and plunge plate in while maintaining the agitation for about four minutes.

175
00:20:08,360 --> 00:20:13,240
And number six is rinse well under running tap water.

176
00:20:13,240 --> 00:20:21,640
And in parentheses, it says any bromide only on the surface can be removed by gently rubbing with ungloved finger.

177
00:20:21,640 --> 00:20:29,800
Plates usually come out of this bath beautifully clear under the green safe light without any special surface deposit.

178
00:20:30,120 --> 00:20:33,000
Final step, sensitizing bath step.

179
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:44,040
The plate can be immersed for one minute in 1% ascorbic acid solution adjusted to pH 5 using a little sodium carbonate or hydroxide.

180
00:20:44,040 --> 00:20:52,760
Alternatively, the well-known triethanolamine pre-swelling technique can be used with the advantage of increasing brightness at a shorter wavelength.

181
00:20:52,760 --> 00:21:00,520
And once again, in parentheses, prolonged settling period may then be necessary, however, to avoid creep while the exposure is being made.

182
00:21:00,520 --> 00:21:03,960
After exposure, the plate is then treated conventionally.

